{
  "requirement": "The requirements index MUST show each requirement's evidence state as Valid, Missing, Determination false, FIXME, or Invalid. Valid MUST mean its determination is true and its record passes the same evidence and reference coverage checks as `habeas::validate()`. A false or FIXME determination MUST have its distinct state when the record passes all other checks. The FIXME state MUST pass validation while retaining its distinct label. Malformed or stale evidence MUST remain Invalid regardless of its determination. Each detail page MUST show any validation findings.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The implementation matches the requirement text and is marked `{impl}`. The tests cover each state and the failure cases that matter.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `EvidenceState` (src/index.rs:126) has the five states. `label()` gives exactly \"Valid\", \"Missing\", \"Determination false\", \"FIXME\" and \"Invalid\".\n- `populate` writes each requirement's state into the index table row. `render_detail_sections` shows the state as a badge on the detail page.\n- `LoadedDetailEvidence::load_path` derives the state from the report that `inspect_repository` produces. That is the same report `habeas::validate_repository()` returns, and `validate()` in src/lib.rs panics on that report's errors. Reference-coverage errors (\"no input covers…\"), reference-set errors, stale requirement text and input hashes, schema errors and missing-evidence errors all start with `evidence/<name>.json:`. `load_path` collects the errors with that prefix.\n- The state is chosen as follows:\n  - **Missing:** the file does not exist.\n  - **Clean record:** if there are no errors, the state follows the determination: true gives Valid, false gives Determination false, FIXME gives FIXME.\n  - **Only the false-determination error:** the state is Determination false.\n  - **Any other error:** the state is Invalid. This covers malformed or stale evidence, and a FIXME record with findings. Invalid applies whatever the determination.\n- A FIXME determination produces no validation error, so it passes validation but keeps its own label. A false determination produces only the \"determination is false\" error, so Determination false is reported only when no other check fails.\n- `render_detail_sections` prints a \"Validation findings\" section whenever the record has errors. That includes the false-determination finding and the missing-record finding.\n\n**Tests**\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` (src/index.rs) covers Valid, Determination false, Invalid from stale requirement text, Invalid from an uncovered `{impl}` reference, and Missing. It also checks the Valid badge on the detail page.\n- `findings_make_false_and_fixme_records_invalid` (src/index.rs) covers the boundary cases:\n  - A clean false record gives Determination false.\n  - A false record with stale text, a malformed extra field, or an uncovered reference gives Invalid.\n  - A clean FIXME record gives FIXME, and a stale FIXME record gives Invalid.\n  - Detail pages show \"Validation findings\" in every state except clean FIXME.\n- tests/outcomes.rs covers three more cases:\n  - A malformed FIXME (`{\"FIXME\": []}`) is Invalid.\n  - A FIXME with a stale input or an uncovered reference is Invalid.\n  - A valid FIXME shows as FIXME in the index and in the detail badge `evidence-FIXME`.\n- `invalid_evidence_detail_remains_readable` covers findings rendering for an invalid record.\n\n**Gaps**\n- None found.\n- No FIXME markers are present.",
  "references": "32af10e9f1acc3238e0ee16a28d200757007609134ecfd760adcd60ed0eb7e93",
